ack gears a little as I mentioned we  work with the primarily with the library  and publishing communities we often  envision in the libraries looking  something like this but in fact many  libraries these days look more like this  the invitee infrastructure for the  library is often one of the most  intensive areas in the in an academic  institution to create and share  information and a lot of library  information is now provided not by the  library it's provided in the cloud via  via service providers the content that  you go when you access something online  isn't actually in the library it's  hosted by the publisher site the  discovery services are hosted by third  parties so increasingly what the library  community is providing to the community  to the patrons is actually not managed  by the library and this runs into  something one of the core principles of  the library community the library  community since the 1930s has had this  as part of their code of ethics about  the commitment that the library  community has to intellectual freedom  and the right of privacy for people who  are accessing library services this was  a response to World War one where people  were actually jailed for their library  search behaviors and ever since then the  library community has actually been very  strong proponents of intellectual  freedom in a way that most of the rest  of the community doesn't really  recognize but they've been staunch  advocates for privacy and how this  relates to our community this  conversation data repositories are  generally not libraries not all data  scientists are librarians  they don't have this background and be a  kind of grounding and it's a little  intellectual freedom one approach to  privacy is i'm going to use as an  example the internet archive i don't  know how many top 10 i think top 20 web  sites on the web erase their server logs  so that they protect the patron the user  privacy i think they stand out in this  regard but i think th