 potentially some  genealogical or genetic link to mental  health disorders well your  great-great-great grandfather was here  in this mental health facility had  congratulations you have a fifty percent  chance of getting Alzheimer's so we're  not going to ensure you anymore I mean  these are the sort of issues when you're  combining multiple data stores and  dealing with personally sensitive  information  another issue and something that I don't  think is discussed much at least in this  context is the issue of the data that's  being generated by the use of these  systems Internet systems are all  tracking behavior all the time we've got  log files about who's using those  systems if you know enough about a field  and you are track and you are looking at  who is gathering information and using  say heart medical data from multiple  different sources and you can see the IP  range and say well that person is coming  from Harvard Medical School I bet that's  probably professor so and so and if  they're looking at this and this and  this if you know enough about the field  you could maybe discern what their  research is maybe jump ahead of them  maybe patent their technology before  they do who is keeping track of these  logs who is making them available who is  using that data I am aware of a couple  organizations who are taking public data  stores replicating them on their own  internal servers which is perfectly fine  I mean they're open data so that's  perfectly fine it's not a problem with  that but the reason that they're doing  that is so that no one is tracking their  behavior if you think about how many of  these data stores are run by government  institutions are the usage logs subject  to FOIA requests they may be so water  policies and practices regarding the  tracking of researcher behavior in these  systems and what are the policies that  those providers have to to manage that  that flow of information another issue i  think is relevant here is the  replication and the interaction with  other systems that is taking place when  accessing data  again this is sort of the the Adobe  Digital Editions issue are you sharing  these data over secure protocols even if  it is even if it does contain personally  identifiable information how secure is  that transmission process another  important information that another  important vector that I don't think we  spend enough time on is the metadata  that's associated with these data sets  what sort of information are we storing  and sharing related to provenance  minimum viable consent what sort of what  was the policy or the legal constraints  of the community where this data was  created Steven mentioned this in his  keynote yesterday I think there's many  issues here about the the international  legal aspects of data sharing and how do  we describe those and the many different  legal frameworks HIPAA and FERPA and EC  privacy laws etc this is a very complex  ecosystem in which we're operating and  the legal environment is incredibly  complex and often contradictory I you  know I think we we are all operating in  in this context and how do we navigate  some of this so those are some of the  issues that we are considering and what  do we do about these to be honest I'm  not exactly sure and that's why I'm  talking to you all but I think the  indisputable fact is the data analysis  data gathering data sharing can be an  extremely useful and powerful tool to  produce novel discoveries and we don't  want to be in a situation where we are  limited either by legislation limited by  patron involvement and engagement in  these systems so how do we support  discoveries how do we do support this  infrastructure  in a way that addresses some of these  privacy concerns one approach that it  nice o is undertaking presently related  specifically to publisher and publisher  library systems and software providers  in our community are looking at is an  initiative to develop some principles  around patron privacy in these systems I  want to shout out to the mellon  Foundation for generously funding this  work it was modest amount of funding but  I really appreciate their their support  of these issues and we've undertaken  about a six-month project which  culminated with an in-person event in  San Francisco back at the end of June  this is the mason center where we met  and spent two days discussing this the  goal of this project is to establish a  framework of principles that describe  how information systems should respect  the privacy of patron data and we are  working on a set of draft principles  that cover these this set of different  topics talking about transparency what's  what sort of policies and descriptions  and statements should be made by  organizations about their their  perspective on privacy issues around  security issues around data collection  and how it should be used the  responsibilities of the actors in the  community to do awareness you know it  doesn't help anyone if you have a  privacy policy that is buried on your  website so how proactive do you need to  be to describe to users I mean we don't  want to go quite so far as they say the  EU approach we're just about every time  I hit a page it's like Oh privacy  privacy privacy which just teaches  people to ignore it but other option you  know other issues are on informed  consent what does it mean to consent how  should data be shared in what way should  it be shared what sort of anonymous  ation issues are dealt with  tation issues around anonymous use so  can I use a system without displaying to  the community that I am Todd carpenter  and I've logged in at a particular time  as well as accountability sort of is  anyone actually following their  procedures I mean google has a privacy  policy has anyone ever audited it do we  know that Google is conformant with its  own policy practices so my issue is can  we do something here similar within the  research data community I thin